// Three reference positions the linker's qualification walk did not visit. Each is a name that has
// to become the qualified identity the declaration now carries, and each failed *differently* from
// the same cause — the walk destructured the node with `..`, so the field was never named and
// therefore never rewritten.
//
//   * `impl Keyed<Grade>` — an `impl`'s trait ARGUMENTS. Both `impl` forms visited the trait name
//     and jumped straight to the methods, so `Grade` here stayed short and the checker reported it
//     as an unknown type, while the identical name one line down (as the method's return type)
//     resolved fine.
//   * `@role(WebRole.Controller)` — nothing anywhere visited a role tag's enum, which the checker
//     looks up by qualified name. And since the grammar takes a bare `Enum.Variant`, spelling it
//     out was not a workaround either: no spelling reached an imported role enum at all.
//   * `n if n > Limits.ceiling()` — a match arm's GUARD, the one arm position that can mention a
//     type other than the one being matched. `Expr::Match` walked the scrutinee, each pattern and
//     each body, and skipped `MatchArm::guard` entirely.
//
// A directory case on purpose: the harness runs a single-file case straight from source text with
// no loader, so namespace qualification never runs for one and a bare `.noe` could not observe any
// of these.
// Its `noeta.toml` is what makes the case a package, and therefore what derives the entry's
// module path: delete the manifest and nothing qualifies, silently.
// expect: stdout "key: Grade.High"
// expect: stdout "rank 50: over"
// expect: stdout "rank 1: under"
// expect: stdout "role: App.main.renderHome=controller"
// expect: exit 0
use App.Lib.WebRole;
use App.Lib.Grade;
use App.Lib.Keyed;
use App.Lib.Limits;

struct Door {
    code: int
    impl Keyed<Grade> {
        fn key(): Grade {
            return Grade.High;
        }
    }
}

@attribute(Function)
@role(WebRole.Controller)
struct Page {
    path: string
}

#[Page("/")]
fn renderHome(): int { return 1; }

fn rank(v: int): string {
    return match v {
        n if n > Limits.ceiling() => "over",
        _ => "under",
    };
}

d = Door { code: 1 };
echo "key: ${d.key()}";
echo "rank 50: ${rank(50)}";
echo "rank 1: ${rank(1)}";

for b in roles_of() {
    name = match b.role {
        WebRole.Controller => "controller",
        _ => "other",
    };
    echo "role: ${b.target}=${name}";
}
